{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at exact clean pushed head 2d3c8a1062e53b51cb5f4c11d67b224c2771b559 on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, answering bc311b36 under my promise c690649c. This succeeds my ec0261df artifact and answers the changes-requested verdict 602a4e57, which I ratified.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG, PLAINLY. The reviewer was right and I want the record to say so in my own words rather than by reference. At ec0261df the owned-tail flush built its output with a limit of byteLimit minus chunkBytes and did not subtract tailBytes, while the whole cloned tail stayed live until the release several lines later. The borrowed-chunk path subtracted both. So a near-budget incompressible tail could sit alongside a near-budget output and the retained peak approached twice the advertised bound. The change did not deliver the aggregate bound it claimed.\n\nIn my ec0261df artifact I described that same window as the retained budget plus one compressed chunk, each term individually within the limit. That was wrong, and it was wrong about a line I had read. The second term was bounded only by byteLimit minus chunkBytes, not by one chunk. I had seen the asymmetry between the two paths and not understood it.\n\nTHE REMEDY, AND WHY THIS ONE. Incremental release rather than blanket reservation. Simply subtracting tailBytes as well would be safe but would fail closed on a large yet compressible tail that would have published without trouble. Instead checkpointLimitWriter now takes a reserve function read live on every write, and flushTail credits each entry content back just before encoding it, so the budget grows as the tail drains. The invariant held at every write instant is that stored chunks, the tail still retained, and the in-progress output fit the limit together. One boundedWriter helper serves the borrowed, owned-tail and marshal paths, so the accounting is shared rather than repeated per path, and no second policy or public configuration surface was added. The unexported test limit seam is unchanged.\n\nWHAT THE ACCOUNTING NOW COUNTS. Chunk buffers at capacity rather than length, with growth performed inside the writer in bounded doubling steps clamped to the live budget, so bytes.Buffer cannot quietly retain more than the budget admits. Tail entries at cloned content length plus flat container charges, 256 bytes for the entry slot including append growth slack and 512 per attachment for map storage, both erring toward over-counting, with a test pinning the entry charge against unsafe.Sizeof so it cannot silently stop covering the struct. A successful flush now releases the backing array instead of truncating it, because a kept array would survive with its charge zeroed. The one residual under-direction, stated in the code rather than hidden, is the allocator size-class padding beneath these figures.\n\nTwo transients sit outside the accounting and are named in the comments: the single entry in hand-off during a flush, bounded by the per-event admission check, and the compressor fixed internal state. Neither grows with history.\n\nI also removed two after-the-fact length checks in the marshal path. That deserves scrutiny, since deleting a bound check is exactly the sort of thing that should not be waved through: every byte there, container header included, now goes through the bounded writer, which refuses when length plus the incoming write would pass the limit, so those checks had become unreachable.\n\nTESTS. Seven mutations, each confirmed applied by reading the diff and confirmed to compile, all killed: reverting the tail reservation, removing the capacity clamp, charging length instead of capacity, removing the accounting entirely, unbounding only the streamed path, keeping the error while skipping the release, and raising the default. The named 4096-event owned-tail peak-budget test the verdict asked for measures the encoded size first, then pins the flush at exactly that budget and at one byte below. Two fixture-validity guards fail loudly if a fixture stops proving what it claims.\n\nOne existing test moved its expected tailBytes to match the honest charges. I read it rather than accept the edit, because a test rewritten to match new behaviour is how a defect gets pinned as correct: it still asserts that three events fit and the fourth overflows and releases. The meaning is unchanged.\n\nGATES, RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD, tree clean. gofmt -l . empty, go vet ./... clean, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with thirty packages ok and no FAIL line anywhere in the log.\n\nTWO HONEST CAVEATS. The exact-budget success branch of the peak test depends on gzip never emitting more than input consumed plus accrued overhead at any prefix. That holds for deflate today and the test is deterministic, but it is a property of the standard library rather than of this code, and a pathological change to flate write batching would need slack there. And during production the working file was again destroyed by a stray checkout and re-applied; the mutation row for the tail reservation comes from a re-run against the commit, not from the lost run.","body":{"commit":"2d3c8a1062e53b51cb5f4c11d67b224c2771b559","path":"internal/kernel"}}